id: decisions/033-attribution-granularity
title: "authored_by says who answers; each mixed claim says how it is known"
kind: decision-frame
record_shape: header
status: draft
authored_by: agent-on-behalf
authored_via: "Claude Code session on Dave's behalf, 2026-09-12; drafted from the JUDGMENTS.md reversal axis 'attribution-granularity'; recommendation is an attributed inference and ratifies nothing"
version: "1.0.0"
updated: 2026-09-12
template_ref: templates/decision.yawn
principal_ref: principal:dave

axis: attribution-granularity
axis_gloss: "whether each individual claim names its source, or attribution is declared once for a whole document"
poles:
  a: per-claim-attribution
  b: document-level-attribution
split: leaning
draft_status: full
leverage: 7.6
leverage_formula: "judgments_resolved(9) x split_weight(0.7) x authored_conflict_bonus(1.2)"

question: >
  Does attribution live once per record (authored_by, source.template) or on
  each claim (epistemic_status and source_refs per line) — and when a record
  carries both, which one may a bot act on?

why_it_matters: >
  The protocol is about attribution and until this week the tree could not
  attribute itself; now a document-level authored_by is required under
  decisions/ and dave/, while the authored rules say human expression and
  machine inference must stay separately labelled on every line, and two
  references cite the same territory at DOI level and at bare-keyword level.
  Once answered, every child Yawn below Dave learns that authored_by answers
  'who stands behind this record' and per-claim tags answer 'how is this line
  known', and that neither substitutes for the other.

state_snapshot:
  description: >
    7 judgments sit at per-claim-attribution (4 authored) and 2 at
    document-level-attribution (0 authored). The repository asserts both
    directions on this axis.
  attributed_to: TAXONOMY.md
  epistemic_status: observed
  source_refs:
    - JUDGMENTS.md
    - TAXONOMY.md

lacuna:
  kind: unselected_choice
  description: >
    Which list keys Dave regards as mixed-voice by nature; and whether the
    eight-dimension statuses.v1 vector belongs on a document or on each snapshot
    (J1131), which the status-dimensionality decision also touches.

options:
  - name: A
    description: >
      rule: Attribution is per claim. Every claim, inference, snapshot and relation
      carries its own attributed_to, epistemic_status and source_refs, as
      readme.yawn's orientation card and core/holarchy.yawn relation_requirements
      already do. Document-level authored_by remains as the answerable party but may
      not stand in for per-claim provenance. Every research anchor names an author,
      year and link or DOI.
    cost: "Per-item tags on every list in core/, records/ and references/ that makes claims; twelve bare anchors in references/scientific-frame.yawn resolved to works; a test that walks list items, not just top-level keys."
    risk: "Records that are one voice throughout (a ledger entry, a template) carry per-line tags that all say the same thing, and the noise hides the lines where attribution matters."
    reversibility: true
    proof_needed: "Extend tests/authored-by.test.mjs: for named list keys (current_state, snapshots, relations, research_anchors, open_questions) every item carries epistemic_status and source_refs or attributed_to; references/scientific-frame.yawn research_anchors each carry at least one of author, doi, url."
    evidence_for:
      - "J0085"
      - "J0586"
      - "J0668"
      - "J0679"
      - "J0800"
      - "J1131"
  - name: B
    description: >
      rule: Attribution is per document. One authored_by and one source block per
      record are sufficient; per-claim tags are optional; examples need not declare
      the template they instantiate; a reference may cite by keyword.
    cost: "Nothing to change beyond the existing tests/authored-by.test.mjs; strike the per-claim tagging in readme.yawn's orientation card or leave it as decoration."
    risk: "A record that mixes a human report and a machine inference under one authored_by lets a bot act on the inference as if it were the report — the failure core/co-orientation-loop.yawn's boundary names first."
    reversibility: true
    proof_needed: "The existing tests/authored-by.test.mjs as-is."
    evidence_for:
      - "J0416"
      - "J0703"
  - name: C
    description: >
      rule: Two layers, each answering a different question, both written down.
      Document-level authored_by names who is answerable for the record and is
      required wherever a record speaks for Dave or as the bot (the current test).
      Per-claim attribution (attributed_to or epistemic_status plus source_refs on
      each item) is required wherever a record mixes voices (human expression with
      machine inference), mixes observers, or states a claim a bot may act on —
      orientation cards, state snapshots, lateral relations, research anchors, open
      questions. A single-voice record attributes once. Every example declares the
      template it instantiates (source.template or template_ref resolving into
      templates/).
    cost: "Extend one test with per-item checks on five named list keys; add source.template to five 2026-08 examples; resolve twelve keyword anchors in references/scientific-frame.yawn to at least a link each."
    risk: "'Mixes voices' is decided per key by the test author, so a new list shape escapes the check until someone names it."
    reversibility: true
    proof_needed: "Extend tests/authored-by.test.mjs with per-item assertions on current_state, snapshots, relations, research_anchors and open_questions; a test that every examples/*.yawn carries source.template or template_ref resolving to a file in templates/; scripts/validate-hub-links.mjs resolves the pointer."
    evidence_for:
      - "J0085"
      - "J0586"
      - "J0679"
      - "J0800"
      - "J0416"
      - "J0703"

recommendation:
  option_ref: C
  confidence: 0.75
  epistemic_status: inferred
  authority: none
  reasoning: >
    Four of the seven per-claim judgments are authored and they are the protocol's
    core commitments: human expression and machine inference separately labelled
    (J0085), every material contribution separately attributable across agents
    (J0586), a per-action accountability map (J0668), contradiction kept explicit
    across observers (J0679). The passing suite's newest test is document-level,
    and its own comment says exactly what it is for: authored_by names who is
    answerable, not who typed it, and 'does not make a record true'. So the two
    layers answer different questions and the suite already has one; the decision
    is to write down that the second is required where voices mix. The two
    document-level judgments are REVEALED omissions — five examples with no
    template pointer, twelve anchors with no author — not a preference for coarse
    attribution. C keeps the existing test, adds the per-item check where the
    authored rules demand it, and does not tag single-voice records line by line.
    A is right in spirit and wrong in scope; B would let a bot act on an inference
    wearing a report's authored_by.

target_condition:
  mode: decide
  proposed_by: agent-on-behalf
  affected_principals:
    - principal:dave
  ratification_status: proposed
  sufficient_when:
    - "tests/authored-by.test.mjs, or a sibling, rejects a current_state, snapshot, relation, research_anchor or open_question item with no epistemic_status and no source_refs or attributed_to, and passes."
    - "Every file under examples/ carries source.template or template_ref resolving into templates/, and a test checks it."
    - "references/scientific-frame.yawn research_anchors each carry at least an author, DOI or link."

choice:
  selected_by: ""
  option_ref: ""
  authority_ref: ""

proof:
  covers:
    - target_condition
    - options
  falsifier: >
    A record carries an observed report and a machine inference under one
    epistemic_status and one authored_by, and npm test stays green; or a new
    example ships with no template pointer.
  status: proposed

evidence_sample:
  - "J0085"
  - "J0586"
  - "J0668"
  - "J0679"
  - "J0800"
  - "J1131"
  - "J0416"
  - "J0703"

boundary:
  - "confidence cannot ratify an objective; this record proposes and Dave decides"
  - "the REVEALED judgments cited here describe what artifacts do, not what Dave chose"

source:
  template: templates/decision.yawn
  axis: TAXONOMY.md
  inventory: JUDGMENTS.md
  id_index: TAXONOMY.md
